<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!--
  Apple's privacy manifest, required for App Store submission.

  The address book lives on the phone (IndexedDB in the app's WebView) and
  BlackBook works with no account at all. What CAN leave, and only when the
  user asks for it:

  - Signing in with a Universal ID: the email and the account id.
  - "Save my book online": the whole book (contacts, tags, notes, to-dos) is
    encrypted ON THE PHONE with AES-GCM-256 under a key derived from a
    passphrase only the user knows (PBKDF2-SHA-256, 600,000 rounds), and only
    the ciphertext, the key-derivation salt and round count, a revision number
    and a timestamp are stored, in one row per account (Supabase,
    public.blackbook_vaults, row-level security on the account id). UNI·SIM
    holds no key and cannot read it, so the book's contents are not "collected"
    in Apple's sense and are not declared; the row itself is tied to the
    account id declared below.
  - While signed in, one "the app was opened" event per launch, tied to the
    account (the SDK's <UsageTracker />). Nothing about the book. Declared as
    product interaction, for analytics, because that is what it is.

  The phone's contacts ("From my contacts", "Import my phone contacts") are
  read on the phone, only when the user asks, and never written to. What is
  read becomes part of the local book and leaves the phone only inside the
  encrypted online copy above, so Contacts is not declared as collected.

  The suite user count (the SDK's presence beat, with its random install id)
  is switched OFF in this app (src/main.tsx, presence: false on the phone), so
  no device identifier is sent and none is declared.

  All of it goes to our own servers. Nothing is used for tracking, and there
  are no third-party analytics or advertising SDKs.

  Required-reason APIs: @capacitor/filesystem (used to hand a CSV export to
  the share sheet) can read file creation and modification dates (its `stat`),
  only on files in the app's own container, and neither it nor its
  IONFilesystemLib pod ships a manifest. Capacitor's core frameworks carry
  their own manifests, and @capacitor/share, @capacitor-community/contacts and
  the SDK's UnisimSdk pod use none of the listed APIs.

  Keep this in step with PRIVACY.md, store-assets/data-safety.json and the
  App Store Connect privacy answers.
-->
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<!-- @capacitor/filesystem's stat: files in the app container. C617.1. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<!-- The sign-in email. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- The account id: sign-in, and the row the encrypted copy is kept in. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- While signed in, one "session.opened" event per launch. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeProductInteraction</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
